# Caddy config for a forwarder box.
#
# @-placeholders are rendered by deploy/install.sh. With no site tunnel
# configured they render empty and Caddy owns :80/:443 directly. When
# SITE_PEER_IP is set, HAProxy takes those ports and Caddy moves to loopback
# 8080/8443 behind it (see deploy/haproxy.cfg for why the split exists).
#
# This file is the WHOLE Caddy config — the box exists to front the forwarder and
# nothing else. If you are adding the forwarder to a box that already runs other
# Caddy sites, do NOT install this; instead copy the two blocks below into that
# box's Caddyfile (the global `on_demand_tls` block merges, and the catch-all
# `https://` site loses to any named site because Caddy matches most-specific
# host first).
#
# Vars come from /etc/forwarder/forwarder.env via the caddy.service.d drop-in
# installed by install.sh.
#
# BEHIND HAPROXY, CADDY ALSO SPEAKS THE PROXY PROTOCOL. :443 is SNI-split in TCP
# mode, so without this Caddy's peer is HAProxy on loopback and every public
# visitor is recorded as 127.0.0.1 — which is exactly what the gallery's serving
# plane measured on every request. The proxy_protocol listener wrapper restores
# the true peer, and `allow` restricts that trust to loopback so nothing
# reachable from outside can assert an address. The `tls` wrapper MUST stay last:
# wrappers run in order, and the PROXY header is in front of the TLS handshake.

{
	# On-demand TLS is GATED: Caddy asks the forwarder before minting a cert, and
	# the forwarder only approves its control host or a hostname a live agent has
	# registered. Without this gate the catch-all below would be an open relay,
	# and anyone could burn the Let's Encrypt rate limit by opening TLS
	# connections with made-up SNI values.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:{$FORWARDER_MGMT_PORT:7001}/ask
	}

	# No `email` here on purpose: an empty placeholder fails `caddy validate`,
	# which would block every deploy on a box that never set one. Add
	# `email you@example.com` by hand if you want Let's Encrypt expiry warnings.
@CADDY_PORTS@
@CADDY_LISTENER_WRAPPERS@
}

# Catch-all. Every HTTPS host that resolves here lands on the forwarder, which
# routes by Host to whichever agent registered that hostname (and handles the
# agents' own control WebSocket on FORWARDER_CONTROL_PATH).
#
# reverse_proxy passes WebSocket upgrades through transparently, so streaming
# guests are never frame-buffered.
https:// {
@CADDY_BIND@
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$FORWARDER_HTTP_PORT:7080}
}
